WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.583
So far every structure had a handful of points that move.

00:00:04.583 --> 00:00:15.680
Now take a bar: fixed to a wall at the left, pulled along its length by a load spread over it, f per unit length, and a load P at the tip.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.593
A bar like this is loaded along its axis, so it only stretches or shortens.

00:00:20.593 --> 00:00:25.820
That's what makes it a bar, as opposed to a beam, which is loaded sideways and bends.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:34.199
Every point along the bar moves a little to the right, by an amount that depends on where it is.

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:35.368
Call it u of x.

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.160
At the wall, u is zero.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.401
So how many unknowns is that?

00:00:40.401 --> 00:00:45.878
Every point has its own displacement: infinitely many.

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:50.340
We can't list them as q one, q two, q three.

00:00:51.273 --> 00:00:52.588
Here's the plan.

00:00:52.588 --> 00:00:57.354
First, find the strain energy and work potential of a bar.

00:00:57.354 --> 00:01:03.353
Then, find a clever way to turn infinitely many unknowns into just a few.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.389
To find the energy in the bar, zoom in on a tiny slice, of length d x.

00:01:10.389 --> 00:01:17.540
Inside it, the material is stretched by a strain epsilon, and carries a stress sigma.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:25.229
For an elastic material, stress and strain are related by Hooke's law: sigma equals E times epsilon.

00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:29.440
On a graph, a straight line from the origin with slope E.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.442
Now connect it to the spring.

00:01:35.442 --> 00:01:38.737
Stress times the slice's area A is a force.

00:01:38.737 --> 00:01:41.879
Strain times its length d x is a stretch.

00:01:41.879 --> 00:01:46.860
So this graph is just the spring's force-stretch graph, rescaled.

00:01:48.524 --> 00:01:53.839
So the slice stores the area of the triangle under the line, times A d x.

00:01:53.839 --> 00:02:02.284
Per unit volume, the energy is just the triangle's area: half base times height, one half, epsilon, times E epsilon.

00:02:02.584 --> 00:02:07.951
We'll call it U star: the strain energy per unit volume.

00:02:07.951 --> 00:02:12.168
It's the same shape as one half K q squared.

00:02:12.168 --> 00:02:17.344
Stiffness, times deformation, squared, times one half.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.950
To get the whole bar's energy, add up every slice.

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:31.159
A slice has volume A, the bar's cross-sectional area, times d x.

00:02:31.159 --> 00:02:36.300
So it stores one half E epsilon squared, times A d x.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.702
What's the strain of a slice?

00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:47.120
Its left face moves u, its right face moves u plus d u, so it grows by d u.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:48.660
Its length is d x.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.340
Change in length over length: d u over d x.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:03:03.800
Add up the slices with an integral from zero to L, and the bar's strain energy is one half, the integral of E A, times d u d x, squared, d x.

00:03:04.133 --> 00:03:11.597
This is the formula the whole rest of the series stands on, so let's make sure it feels familiar.

00:03:11.597 --> 00:03:16.368
Stiffness, times how much it deforms, squared, times one half.

00:03:16.368 --> 00:03:20.293
Exactly the spring's formula, spread along the bar.

00:03:20.627 --> 00:03:36.457
In fact, if the bar stretches evenly, so its tip moves q, then d u d x is q over L everywhere, and U becomes one half, E A over L, q squared.

00:03:36.457 --> 00:03:40.387
A spring with stiffness E A over L.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:47.290
And if the bar just slides along without stretching, d u d x is zero, and so is U.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.600
No stretch, no stored energy.

00:03:50.833 --> 00:03:56.395
The work potential is load times displacement, as always.

00:03:56.395 --> 00:04:03.713
The tip load P acts where the bar moves u of L, so it gives P times u at L.

00:04:04.013 --> 00:04:07.080
The spread-out load acts everywhere.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:13.213
A slice d x carries f d x, and moves u, so add up f u d x along the bar.

00:04:13.546 --> 00:04:21.955
So W equals P u at L, plus the integral of f u d x.

00:04:21.955 --> 00:04:27.066
And Pi is U minus W, as before.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.910
But here's the problem.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:31.734
Pi now depends on a whole function, u of x.

00:04:31.734 --> 00:04:35.870
We can't take a partial derivative with respect to every point.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:36.920
We need a trick.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.042
Here's the trick.

00:04:39.042 --> 00:04:42.840
Guess the shape of u of x, but leave a number in it to adjust.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:48.600
Then Pi depends only on that number, and we're back to finding the bottom of an ordinary bowl.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.158
Let's try it.

00:04:50.158 --> 00:04:53.641
Only the spread load f, no tip load.

00:04:53.641 --> 00:04:56.157
Assume u equals a times x.

00:04:56.157 --> 00:05:00.995
It's zero at the wall, so it respects the support.

00:05:00.995 --> 00:05:03.220
Then d u d x is just a.

00:05:03.553 --> 00:05:13.750
U is one half, E A a squared, integrated over the length: one half E A a squared L.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:20.753
W is the integral of f times a x: one half f a L squared.

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:29.273
So Pi of a is one half E A a squared L, minus one half f a L squared.

00:05:29.273 --> 00:05:34.967
A parabola in a: a bowl, just like the spring's.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:43.022
Set its derivative to zero: E A a L minus one half f L squared equals zero.

00:05:43.022 --> 00:05:46.000
So a equals f L over two E A.

00:05:46.387 --> 00:05:49.803
Why is that the best straight line?

00:05:49.803 --> 00:05:53.708
The true shape has the lowest Pi of all.

00:05:53.708 --> 00:06:01.907
So among straight lines, the one with the lowest Pi is the one closest to the truth.

00:06:01.907 --> 00:06:06.787
Its tip moves a times L: f L squared over two E A.

00:06:07.187 --> 00:06:11.603
Pay close attention, because this is the idea finite elements are built on.

00:06:11.603 --> 00:06:18.787
Assume a shape that respects the supports, with a few unknown numbers in it, and the infinite problem becomes a small one.

00:06:19.933 --> 00:06:21.899
How good is that guess?

00:06:21.899 --> 00:06:24.975
This simple bar has an exact answer.

00:06:24.975 --> 00:06:34.888
Cut it at x: the piece beyond the cut carries f times L minus x, so the strain there is f times L minus x, over E A.

00:06:34.888 --> 00:06:42.493
Integrate that, and you get u of x equals f over E A, times L x minus x squared over two.

00:06:42.793 --> 00:06:47.059
At the tip, the exact answer is f L squared over two E A.

00:06:47.059 --> 00:06:49.753
Exactly what our straight line gave.

00:06:50.053 --> 00:06:54.734
Your turn: pause and do checkpoint four point one in your guide.

00:06:54.734 --> 00:06:57.733
Compare the two at the middle of the bar.

00:07:00.933 --> 00:07:05.539
At the middle, the straight line gives one quarter f L squared over E A.

00:07:05.539 --> 00:07:07.713
The exact answer is three eighths.

00:07:07.713 --> 00:07:10.783
The line sits too low everywhere inside the bar.

00:07:10.783 --> 00:07:14.173
The tip matching was a lucky coincidence, not a rule.

00:07:16.062 --> 00:07:20.070
That's the honest truth about assumed shapes: they're approximate.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:22.317
A better shape gives a better answer.

00:07:22.317 --> 00:07:26.142
But choosing one good shape for a whole real structure is hard.

00:07:27.565 --> 00:07:32.592
The course slides use the same trick on a beam, so let's see it once.

00:07:32.592 --> 00:07:39.221
A beam of length L, stiffness E I, supported at both ends, with a point load at the middle.

00:07:39.221 --> 00:07:45.485
The slides call that load W; we'll call it P, so it doesn't clash with work potential.

00:07:47.577 --> 00:07:57.675
Measure z along the beam, and let v be how far it sags, positive downward, the way the load points.

00:07:57.675 --> 00:08:01.857
Call the middle point B, and its sag v B.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:08.590
Assume the sag is a sine hump: v equals v B times sine of pi z over L.

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:13.985
It's zero at both supports, and peaks at v B in the middle.

00:08:13.985 --> 00:08:16.270
It respects the supports.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:29.101
For a beam, the deformation is the bending, the curvature, and the strain energy is the integral of M squared over two E I, with M equal to minus E I times v double prime.

00:08:29.101 --> 00:08:33.570
Same pattern: one half, stiffness, times deformation squared.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:35.331
Put the sine in.

00:08:35.331 --> 00:08:41.723
Its second derivative is minus v B, pi over L squared, times the sine.

00:08:41.723 --> 00:08:50.670
Square it and integrate, and U comes out to pi to the fourth, E I, v B squared, over four L cubed.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.595
Pi is that, minus P times v B.

00:08:53.595 --> 00:09:02.258
Set its derivative with respect to v B to zero: v B equals two P L cubed over pi to the fourth E I.

00:09:02.258 --> 00:09:07.770
That's zero point zero two zero five three, P L cubed over E I.

00:09:08.104 --> 00:09:15.304
Beam theory's exact answer is P L cubed over forty eight E I, which is zero point zero two zero eight three.

00:09:15.304 --> 00:09:18.904
A one-line guess, within about one and a half percent.

00:09:20.165 --> 00:09:29.045
So assumed shapes work, but one shape for the whole structure is hard to choose, and our single straight line missed the curve inside the bar.

00:09:29.465 --> 00:09:33.476
Here's the way out, and it's the whole idea of finite elements.

00:09:33.476 --> 00:09:35.450
Chop the bar into short pieces.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:39.524
In each piece, use the simplest shape there is, a straight line.

00:09:39.524 --> 00:09:41.625
The pieces join at shared points.

00:09:41.898 --> 00:09:48.479
And now the unknowns are just the displacements of those shared points: q one, q two, q three, q four.

00:09:48.479 --> 00:09:51.898
A handful of numbers again, exactly like our springs.

00:09:52.198 --> 00:09:55.782
Lots of short straight lines can follow any curve.

00:09:55.782 --> 00:10:01.158
And each piece is so simple that we can work out its energy once, for good.

00:10:01.498 --> 00:10:08.664
So, stepping back: the strain energy of a bar is one half the integral of E A times strain squared.

00:10:08.664 --> 00:10:11.849
Assume a shape, and Pi becomes a bowl again.

00:10:11.849 --> 00:10:15.178
Next time: one element, worked out completely.
